Five Legal Judgments:
1. Lecturing on and reading the statutes and ordinances;
2. Market officials evaluating the prices of goods;
3. Privately bypassing or fraudulently passing through passes and fords;
4. Deliberately committing an offense upon hearing that there will be an act of grace and amnesty;
5. Officials and lesser functionaries of the authorities not residing in their public quarters.

Third Session
Five Policy Questions

Question: The Book of Documents sets forth constant study, and the Book of Odes sings of continuous bright virtue; even in the flourishing eras of Tang, Yu, and the Three Dynasties praised in the annals and biographies, each ruler likewise had teachers from whom he received instruction. Thus, among those who rule over men, there has never been one who did not exert himself in learning and yet was able to manifest himself in enlightened sageliness. In later ages, scholarly learning fell into neglect and deficiency, and sovereign virtue rarely flourished. When on occasion there was a ruler who delighted in drawing close to Confucian gentry and letting his spirit roam among books and scrolls, the histories of former times regarded it as an admired tale. Take, for instance, summoning scholars to lecture in the Stone Canal Pavilion (Shiqu ge) and personally pronouncing imperial decisions upon attending; lecturing on and discussing the principles of the Classics without sleeping until midnight; carefully selecting men of literary learning to take turns on night duty in the palace; ushering scholars in to discuss with them the words and deeds of antiquity, laying down the scrolls only after the hours from si [9–11 a.m.] to shen [3–5 p.m.] had passed; or inscribing the "Against Luxurious Ease" ("Wuyi") chapter of the Book of Documents upon a screen—these are the most conspicuously celebrated examples. Are they the same as what the ancient emperors and kings called learning? Or do they differ in flower and fruit? …
